{
  "format": "wspradar.config",
  "schema_version": 1,
  "profile": {
    "id": "vanhamel_rx_calibration",
    "title": {
      "en": "#4 Dr. Vanhamel et al. RX Chain Calibration: Calibrating a 1.2 dB Receiver Offset"
    },
    "description": {
      "en": "[Publication: Vanhamel, J., Machiels, W., & Lamy, H. (2022). Using the WSPR Mode for Antenna Performance Evaluation and Propagation Assessment on the 160-m Band](https://doi.org/10.1155/2022/4809313)\n\nBefore comparing two antennas, first check whether the receiving equipment introduces a difference of its own. This demo explores **receiver-chain calibration**: measuring a systematic difference in reported signal-to-noise ratio (SNR) so that it can be accounted for in a subsequent antenna comparison. The receiver chain includes the receiver, filters, cables and signal-processing settings.\n\nReconstructs a seven-day receiver-chain calibration baseline for 6–13 February 2021 with no SNR correction applied. The paper describes calibration with both receiver chains connected to the same antenna. WSPRadar compares **ON4AWM0 as Target** with **ON4AWM1 as Reference** on **160 m**, using the archive window from **6 February 06:00 to 13 February 06:00 UTC** and transmitters within **2,500 km**. In the paper’s shared-antenna arrangement, a persistent difference reveals an offset between the receiving chains that would otherwise influence the antenna comparison.\n\nWSPRadar matches reports of the **same transmitting station and reported locator in the same two-minute WSPR cycle**, decoded by both receivers. Each matched observation is a **Joint Spot**. The difference is **ΔSNR = ON4AWM0 − ON4AWM1**: positive values mean that ON4AWM0 reported a higher SNR, negative values mean that ON4AWM1 did, and 0 dB means equal reported SNR. Reports decoded by only one receiver cannot provide this paired difference.\n\nGo to **Segment Inspector → Benchmark Evidence**, keep all distance ranges and directions selected, and read the **Mean** for **Joint-Spot Δ SNR**. The 1,143 same-cycle joint reports from the ten transmitters passing the ≥50-joint-report threshold average +1.214 dB for ON4AWM0 − ON4AWM1, displayed as +1.2 dB; each transmitter has a +1.0 dB median. The threshold means that each included transmitter must contribute at least 50 matched observations during the selected week.\n\nCompare this with **Station Medians (Δ SNR)**, where each transmitter contributes one median, here **+1.0 dB**. The **+1.2 dB Joint-Spot mean** averages all 1,143 matched observations and is the value compared with the paper’s reported average.\n\nAdding **+1.2 dB** to Reference receiver ON4AWM1’s SNR would bring the reconstructed mean difference close to **0 dB**. This demo leaves the correction at **0.0 dB** so that the original offset remains visible."
    }
  },
  "settings": {
    "core_parameters": {
      "analysis_direction": "rx",
      "callsign": "ON4AWM0",
      "qth": "JO20OT",
      "band": "160m",
